#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:5
#: 1fd40ab3e872435c9628db2411eef6da
msgid "Navigation Tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:8
#: f081ca742e214d3bb49853f011a8cc70
msgid "Navigation Tuning with the L1 Controller"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:10
#: a266fb19bb53489783361840e499e7c7
msgid "Starting with Plane 2.72 a new navigation controller called the \"L1 controller\" was introduced. This controller produces much more accurate flight paths both for waypoints and loiter than the previous crosstrack and PID controller."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:15
#: 01f7cd2e16184e1ca02a6ec8edec72bf
msgid "Tuning the navigation controller usually involves adjusting one key parameter, called :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>`. The default is 20, which results in reasonable turns for most R/C airframes. A smaller value for :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` will lead to more aggressive navigation (sharper corners). A larger value will lead to gentler navigation. You can additionally adjust the :ref:`NAVL1_DAMPING<NAVL1_DAMPING>` and :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>` for further tuning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:23
#: b1195b9fc49e4cf5a9c362d537b834be
msgid "Steps to tuning the L1 controller"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:25
#: beeb6a334d28442198578d0986c3ffae
msgid "Make sure you have already tuned the roll and pitch controller, correctly trimmed your plane, and have ensured the plane does not gain or lose altitude in turns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:28
#: 128ff67138744b7abcf5a1d9d94105a8
msgid "Make sure you have setup :ref:`ROLL_LIMIT_DEG<ROLL_LIMIT_DEG>` to an appropriate value for the bank angle you are comfortable with your plane flying without stalling. For slow flying electric gliders a value of around 5000 (50 degrees) is a good start. For fast flying aerobatic planes you may wish to use a larger value, such as 6500 (65 degrees)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:33
#: 042583eebae44a4484a78fbd424f5e18
msgid "Setup a rectangular mission for your plane to fly, with a loop so it repeats the mission continuously. Ensure that the size of the mission is small enough that you will have a good view of the aircraft at all times"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:37
#: 239fe13bffdb48f493a625e90c62de30
msgid "Set :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` to the default value of 17, and :ref:`NAVL1_DAMPING<NAVL1_DAMPING>` to the default value of 0.75. Set :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>` to the distance your plane would fly in 2 seconds at cruise speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:40
#: 044b97ea058f40158b14a6659c21e8f7
msgid "Takeoff and put the plane in AUTO using your transmitter switch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:41
#: 8418b3f7b89447228995139170f6467e
msgid "Observe the behaviour of the plane in the turns. If it turns too slowly then reduce :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` by 5. If it is \"weaving\" after a turn then increase :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` by 1 or 2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:44
#: 4d5a8536074a4fcda026f9b0913c7fc9
msgid "If you are tuning for maximum performance, once you have completed the tuning of :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` you can increment :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>` by 1 and then modify :ref:`NAVL1_DAMPING<NAVL1_DAMPING>` in steps of 0.05 to get the response you want. Do not decrease :ref:`NAVL1_DAMPING<NAVL1_DAMPING>` too much - it is unlikely you will need a value below 0.6."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:51
#: 24c1daac638c41debbd814d376f2a049
msgid "Tuning waypoint transition behaviour"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:53
#: 732144c0f47f49c6aab5b5831a02e7eb
msgid "To control whether Plane flies through a waypoint, then turns afterwards or turns before the waypoint so that it neatly lines up for the next leg of the mission you should adjust the waypoint radius via the :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>` parameter. If you want Plane to fly through the waypoint then set the :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>` to a small number, perhaps 10 meters. If you want Plane to turn before the waypoint so that it lines up with the next leg of the mission then set :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>` to a larger number. A good starting value is to set it equal to the turn radius your aircraft can easily achieve. This will normally be the same as your loiter radius :ref:`WP_LOITER_RAD<WP_LOITER_RAD>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:64
#: 9f63413954cd4d459c23a86b1bb953b0
msgid "L1 Controller Background & Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:66
#: 680e44be2cfd4a71b6fe71bf1df0297c
msgid "The L1 controller concept is based on the following technical paper:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:74
#: 472e84af541349a5806858d49977a51d
msgid "This was the basis for Brandon Jones’ original pull request: https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/pull/101"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:77
#: 42c072e553ea4b8c9e082a0c48ef570e
msgid "These algorithms were subsequently modified by Paul Riseborough with the following changes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:80
#: c27355525cff4b008dff59e1c5ac867b
msgid "The L1 length was calculated dynamically to enable a constant period for the tracking loop to be specified by the user and to enable the navigation loop gain to automatically adjust for changes in aircraft ground speed. Achieving a constant period for the guidance loop gives a consistent response across a range of airspeeds and enables the tuning parameter to be related to the time required to roll the aircraft and measure its response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:87
#: 2c763ee43c2b4348ba88ebb6789f86cd
msgid "The guidance gain was changed from a fixed value of 2 to be calculated based on the :ref:`NAVL1_DAMPING<NAVL1_DAMPING>` value set by the user. This enabled additional damping to be specified to compensate for delays in the velocity measurement and aircraft roll response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:91
#: d99103fccf024d18849eb6405e55c9bd
msgid "A complementary filter fusing GPS velocities, airspeed and aircraft heading was used to estimate the ground speed vector. This enabled the accuracy of the GPs velocity to be taken advantage of, without the limitations imposed by its inherent latency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:95
#: 17cfeb7ace96446db12c1451f65ffdba
msgid "The track capture algorithm was modified to enable explicit control over the track capture angle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:97
#: 5feb0f5d7e9446028b0b4b3fbb5d9e9a
msgid "The waypoint circle tracking algorithm used during RTL, GUIDED and LOITER modes was modified to use a modified PD control law rather than the L1 control law. This was necessary to enable small loiter radius’ to be flown in combination with larger values of :ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD<NAVL1_PERIOD>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/navigation-tuning.rst:102
#: 0f0b4a2d59794d398ed3d5037c5f3eb0
msgid "The distance from the next waypoint to start the turn onto the next track segment was modified to use the L1 length dynamically calculated by the algorithm, but constrained to be no greater than :ref:`WP_RADIUS<WP_RADIUS>`. This enabled the user to select whether they would rather fly through the waypoint and then turn, or turn early and smoothly intercept the next track."
msgstr ""
